# SignalScope UML Class Diagram

The diagram uses Mermaid UML-style syntax. Paste it into a Mermaid-compatible editor and export it as an image or PDF for submission.

```mermaid
classDiagram
    class SignalBuffer~T~ {
        -T* data_
        -size_t size_
        -size_t capacity_
        -bool sorted_
        -updateSortedState() void
        +SignalBuffer(capacity)
        +SignalBuffer(other)
        +~SignalBuffer()
        +operator=(other) SignalBuffer&
        +append(value) bool
        +operator+=(value) SignalBuffer&
        +sortData() void
        +isSorted() bool
        +empty() bool
        +size() size_t
        +capacity() size_t
        +operator[](index) T&
        +data() const T*
        +operator<<(out, buffer) ostream&
    }

    class SearchReport {
        -string algorithm_
        -int index_
        -long long comparisons_
        -long long timeMicroseconds_
        -string message_
        +SearchReport(algorithm, index, comparisons, time, message)
        +algorithm() string
        +index() int
        +comparisons() long long
        +timeMicroseconds() long long
        +message() string
        +found() bool
        +operator<<(out, report) ostream&
    }

    class SearchAlgorithms {
        <<template functions>>
        +linearSearch~T~(values, size, target) SearchReport
        +binarySearch~T~(values, size, target, sorted) SearchReport
        +ternarySearch~T~(values, size, target, sorted) SearchReport
        +interpolationSearch~T~(values, size, target, sorted) SearchReport
    }

    SearchAlgorithms ..> SearchReport : creates reports
    SearchAlgorithms ..> SignalBuffer : receives buffer data
```

## Design notes

- `-` indicates private members and `+` indicates public members.
- `SignalBuffer<T>` owns the dynamic array and tracks both current size and capacity.
- `SearchReport` stores the algorithm name, result index, comparison count, elapsed time, and an optional validation message.
- The search algorithms are function templates, so they can be instantiated for both `int` and `double`.
